        <h2 id="price-tiers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          Price Tiers: What You Actually Get at Each Budget Level
        </h2>
        <p>
          Treadmill pricing isn't linear, and understanding what actually
          changes as you move up in price helps explain why our category
          winners land where they do. Below roughly $1,000, you're mostly
          paying for basic reliability: a motor that won't strain under
          normal walking and light jogging, a deck that folds without
          drama, and a console that covers the essentials without
          unnecessary complexity. The Horizon T101 exemplifies this tier
          well, delivering dependable performance without chasing features
          that would push the price higher.
        </p>
        <p>
          Between roughly $1,000 and $2,000, motors get stronger, decks get
          longer, and cushioning systems become genuinely sophisticated
          rather than a marketing bullet point. This is where the Sole F65
          and NordicTrack Commercial 1750 both compete, and it's arguably
          the most crowded, most competitive price bracket in the entire
          market, which is good news for shoppers since competition keeps
          manufacturers honest about build quality.
        </p>
        <p>
          From roughly $2,000 to $5,000, you start paying for specialization:
          extreme incline ranges, oversized touchscreens, and premium
          cushioning systems built for a specific use case rather than
          general-purpose fitness. The NordicTrack X22i's incline range and
          the early entries into commercial-grade construction both live in
          this bracket. Above $5,000, you're paying for true commercial-duty
          engineering, AC motors, reinforced fixed frames, and warranties
          built around all-day gym use, which is overkill for casual home
          training but genuinely justified for heavy, multi-user households.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's worth noting that price and category winner don't always
          scale together the way you'd expect. The most expensive machine in
          this roundup, the Matrix Fitness T7xe, wins its category not
          because it's the priciest option available at any budget, but
          because it's the strongest performer specifically among machines
          built to true commercial duty-cycle standards. Similarly, the
          least expensive winner here, the XTERRA TRX3500, earns its badge
          by being the strongest folding treadmill we've tested, not simply
          the cheapest one. Category badges reward being the best answer to
          a specific question, not the highest or lowest price point on the
          shelf.
        </p>
        <p>
          One more pricing nuance worth flagging: sale events and seasonal
          discounts can shift the effective price of a machine by several
          hundred dollars without changing anything about the hardware
          itself. We price-check our category winners periodically and note
          typical street price ranges rather than list prices for exactly
          this reason, since a machine that looks expensive at MSRP can
          become a clear value pick during a promotional period, and vice
          versa. Always compare current pricing against the ranges listed
          here before assuming a category winner is out of your budget.
        </p>

